{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00455 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Loratadine, a second-generation antihistamine, is implicated in the pathogenesis of the common cold through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Loratadine may influence the development of nasopharyngitis, a common precursor to the common cold, by modulating inflammatory responses. This is supported by the indication of Loratadine in nasopharyngitis, which in turn is linked to the common cold via multiple indirect pathways. The presence of multiple independent pathways—each involving Loratadine and a different intermediate—strengthens the hypothesis that Loratadine may exert a role in the inflammatory cascade that leads to the common cold. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a plausible mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The integration of these pathways suggests that Loratadine may act through a mechanism that affects inflammatory mediators or immune responses, potentially contributing to the onset or severity of the common cold.",
